# Claude Code workflow reference

Env-specific facts and decision records only. Generic harness behavior (commands,
flags, modes) is injected into every session or answerable live via the
claude-code-guide agent — it does not belong here. If behavior seems changed,
verify with that agent and update this file.

## Models (last verified 2026-09-07)

- Global model is pinned in settings (`model` key) from the chezmoi repo as
  `{{ .agents.claude.defaults.model }}`; `/model` changes only the current
  session. The pin exists to hold the current Opus in its long-context variant
  (`[1m]`); it is not the top tier — Fable 5.1 sits above it, explicit-selection
  only (`/model fable` resolved to Fable 5.1 on 2026-09-07). Pin kept on Opus 5
  at the 2026-09-07 audit: Anthropic's "low-effort Fable 5.1 beats Opus on cost
  per task" claim is API pricing, not Max-plan metering.
- Pins use explicit model IDs, never bare aliases: aliases re-resolve across
  CLI releases (`opus`/`sonnet` float to the newest generation at v2.1.219+,
  and `haiku` was observed resolving to sonnet-5), so an alias pin can silently
  change tier. The agent-instructions-audit skill live-probes every pin.
- Subagent tiers are relative capability, not fixed model names; the named
  specialists are pinned per tier in `.chezmoidata/agents.yaml`
  (`subagent_tiers.claude`) and do NOT follow `/model`. Only unpinned/built-in
  subagents inherit the session model; a subagent naming an unavailable model
  silently falls back to the inherited one. Tier routing rules live in CLAUDE.md
  (lowest tier likely to one-shot the task).
- Subscription limits (Max plan; re-checked 2026-09-07 against the costs doc):
  a rolling 5-hour session window plus a shared weekly pool, "shared across all
  models"; the errors doc still names Opus and Sonnet family sub-limit messages.
  The earlier notes that Fable draws the shared pool under a 50% ceiling and
  that "Opus 5 consumes the budget fastest; Haiku barely dents it" are no longer
  in the costs doc — unverified, re-check against the errors doc or support
  article next time a limit binds, and reconcile the Sonnet sub-limit against
  `/usage`. Leaf work on small/mid subagents still spares the pool — provided
  the pin actually ran the cheap tier.
- Fable 5.1 is escalation-only (hardest long-horizon architecture/synthesis —
  current Opus is near-parity on ordinary coding at normal metering): a grown
  session `/model`-bumped into Fable re-meters its whole context against the
  shared pool at Fable's weight every turn — the rationale behind the CLAUDE.md
  intake rule. Fable 5.1's own quirks (whole-file rewrites, fewer progress
  updates, under-batched tool calls) are projected in CLAUDE.md or carried by
  the harness prompt; Opus 5's (over-delegation, over-verification) showed no
  signal in the 2026-09-07 sweep — Opus 5 and Fable sessions delegated at the
  same rate (3.8% vs 3.5% of tool calls) under the model-neutral rule.
- `implementer` subagent (added 2026-08-28): mid-tier executor for
  spec-complete mechanical write work. Rationale is weighted metering plus
  grown-context re-metering — a bounded edit delegated out of a grown `[1m]`
  session avoids re-sending the whole context at Opus rates, independent of
  sub-limit structure. Uptake by ISO week: 1 (W35), 7 (W36), 16 (W37, two
  days) — growing; rework signal still not measurable from metadata.
- `researcher` subagent (added 2026-09-08): the 2026-09-07 roster gap (113 of
  183 `general-purpose` dispatches in 30 days carrying a hand-picked `sonnet`,
  mostly web research) had grown to 133 of 217 by 09-08 — a scoped mid-tier
  read-only preset with web + Exa + context7. Revisit trigger: next sweep
  checks that `general-purpose`+`sonnet` research dispatches moved to it.
- Self-review vs codex-review (decision 2026-09-08): both rules fired on the
  same high-stakes set from 2026-08-27, and the transcript sweep showed the
  Codex review displacing the fresh-eyes subagent pass — reviewer-subagent
  dispatch in editing sessions fell 56% → 30% → 35% (W35–W37) as codex-review
  rose 37% → 61% → 85%, ~70% of codex-review sessions dispatching no reviewer.
  Web research (sources verified, listed in the baseline why) found the
  fresh-context same-model pass is the measured lever and cross-model review
  additive but unmeasured, so the projection now says "in addition to,
  launched alongside".
- Fresh-eyes sweep (2026-09-09, 30 days, 341 Claude sessions with >=3 writes):
  the 09-08 revisit trigger fired and failed — reviewer-subagent dispatch is
  29%, flat against the 30%/35% it was set from, while `codex-review` ran in
  43% of editing sessions and **41% had no review pass of any kind**. The
  fresh-eyes rule itself fired in only 20 dispatches (~6%): 10 to
  `general-purpose` (write-capable; 4 carrying a hand-picked `sonnet`, 4
  inheriting, 1 opus, 1 fable), 8 to diff/language reviewers whose contract
  cannot report an omission, 2 to `Explore`/`researcher`. That is what
  `spec-reviewer` (added 2026-09-09, `tier: inherit` as a capability floor)
  answers. Note it fixes the shape of the ~6% that fire, not the firing rate —
  the low fire rate is the larger open defect and belongs to the trigger, not
  the fleet. Revisit trigger: `spec-reviewer` dispatch count is now a direct
  measure of the rule (no more reviewer-rate proxy) — next sweep expects it
  above the 20/30d the unnamed pass managed, and re-checks the 41% no-review
  figure.
- Codex pin caveat for pinned reviewers (2026-09-09): the Codex subagent
  section in `private_dot_codex/docs/harness.md.tmpl` is expired and
  openai/codex#32587 is still open — a spawned child can silently inherit the
  parent's model/effort instead of its pinned config. `tier: inherit` is the
  one setting that regression cannot break, which is a second reason the
  fresh-eyes floor is expressed as inherit rather than a fixed tier.
- If Codex is available, `codex-review` / `codex-advisor` are read-only Codex
  consultation for high-risk diffs or stuck calls; Claude and Codex meter on
  independent windows, so read-work can offload to Codex — write-work means driving
  the task with Codex yourself. Keep decision ownership in Claude.
- Codex trigger recalibrated to risk-or-breadth (decision 2026-09-03, from a
  full transcript sweep): the 2026-08-27 size-based trigger ("3+ files,
  unprompted") drove `codex-review` from ~3–4 to ~90 firings/week while the
  needs-attention rate fell from ~4% (11/282 verdict mentions) to ~0.8%
  (2/265), with ~2/3 of reviews returning zero-finding "approve"; every
  surviving catch sat in a high-stakes category (auth, data loss, migration
  safety). External practitioner consensus and Anthropic's yield-by-size data
  agree: gate on risk first, breadth second, never blanket. 2026-09-08
  re-measure (the 09-07 "~1/day" reading was an activity artifact — 9–10
  sessions/day on 09-05/06): among sessions with 3+ edits, codex-review fired
  in 37% (W35), 61% (W36), 85% (W37, 2 days: 22 firings across 21–23 active
  sessions/day); 17 completed reviews that week, ~15 returning P1/P2-labelled
  findings. The bridge returns prose, not a JSON verdict, so the old
  needs-attention regex measures nothing; the survived-findings rate (findings
  the session actually fixed) is the yield measure and is not yet counted.
  Decision 2026-09-08: keep the trigger (Codex itself objected that labels are
  not survivals). Revisit trigger: next `agent-instructions-audit` sweep counts
  survived findings per review from the adjudication text; if under ~2 in 10
  reviews, tighten the breadth clause or drop the unprompted clause.

## Codex transport: owned MCP bridge (decision 2026-09-05)

- `codex mcp-server` (the previous transport) was deprecated in codex-cli
  0.149.1 with removal announced, so the `codex` MCP entry now spawns
  `scripts/codex-bridge.mjs` from the chezmoi source repo, wrapping the stable
  `codex exec` / `codex exec review` CLI (verified present at 0.153.1). Tools:
  `review`, `advise`, `reply`; invocation fixed to read-only sandbox, never
  approvals, high reasoning — callers pick scope only, never sandbox flags.
  `review` states the scope inside the prompt when custom instructions are
  given: the CLI rejects a PROMPT combined with `--base`/`--uncommitted`
  (verified at 0.153.4).
- MCP transport kept deliberately: the bridge is harness-spawned outside the
  Bash sandbox (the `~/.codex` deny stays fully intact), and its rendered
  allow rule (`mcp__codex`, from `auto_approve_tools`) makes the tools
  prompt-free in plan mode — verified live 2026-09-05. Plan mode blocks MCP
  tools as a class unless allow-listed (`readOnlyHint` is ignored; upstream
  issue #12368 closed "not planned"), and Bash has no plan-mode allowlist
  exemption at all, which rules out every Bash-based transport (plain
  `codex exec` calls and the plugin alike).

## Codex plugin (not installed — reaffirmed 2026-09-05)

- OpenAI's official plugin (`openai/codex-plugin-cc`, first dissected at
  v1.0.6) stays uninstalled, on updated grounds: its command paths spawn the
  experimental `codex app-server` through sandboxed Bash — still blocked by
  the `~/.codex` deny, never prompt-free in plan mode (see above), and riding
  a protocol OpenAI documents as changeable without notice. The bridge covers
  review + advice on the stable `exec` interface with no sandbox carve-out.
  The extras remain excluded by doctrine (write-capable rescue, stop-review
  gate, session transfer, background job manager). Reconsider only if the
  `codex exec` interface itself breaks AND the sandbox/plan-mode constraints
  change.

## pi (side-by-side trial vs codex, started 2026-09-05)

- pi (`earendil-works/pi`, adopted at v0.85.0) runs interactive GPT sessions
  alongside codex until one wins the trial; ChatGPT-subscription OAuth in
  third-party OSS harnesses is OpenAI-sanctioned (Codex for OSS program names
  pi; last verified 2026-09-05) and draws the same plan quota as codex.
- pi ships no permission system: the managed workflow extension leases a
  per-tool srt (`@anthropic-ai/sandbox-runtime`) worker for every
  `workspace_*` call, its profile rendered from `.chezmoidata/agents.yaml`,
  and the `pi` zsh function launches the pinned CLI with that extension; the
  workflow's own plan mode supplies read-only planning (verified 2026-09-09).
  `~/.pi/agent/settings.json` is deliberately unmanaged — pi rewrites it
  itself; wholesale ownership would revert `/settings` and Ctrl+S saves on
  every apply (revisit with a `modify_` merge script if pi wins).
- Trial decision trigger: revisit at the next `agent-instructions-audit`, or
  sooner once one harness clearly owns all interactive GPT sessions — then
  demote or drop the loser (codex keeps the review-backend role regardless).
  2026-09-07 audit: two days in, not measurable from Claude transcripts.
  2026-09-08 audit: the pi store is now in the sweep (15 sessions in three days,
  all in this repo — self-development, not a usage signal); the Codex store
  was denied at the permission gate. Trigger fired, decision still yours: not
  decidable from transcripts while pi is only used to build pi.
